      "text": "The sharpest part of the week came on Friday. Reuters, citing two people briefed on the matter, reported that SEC enforcement staff are examining potential accounting issues at Nvidia. The SEC declined to comment and Nvidia did not immediately respond. The stock was already down more than 6% before the report, according to Reuters. Afterward it was down about 13%, per LSEG data. Nvidia closed at $125.53, a day change of −14.0%, on volume of 607.8 million shares. That is about 262% above its three-month daily average of 167.9 million. Heavy trading for a stock this size.\n\nReuters also pointed to guarantees Nvidia has provided for data-center projects that use its equipment. Critics say such arrangements can make it harder to assess the quality of chipmakers' revenue. The reporting describes an examination of potential issues, not a conclusion.\n\nSeparately, Decrypt, citing Reuters, noted that U.S. authorities have been investigating Megaspeed, a Singapore-based former large Nvidia customer, since 2024 over possible export-control violations. That is a different matter, and the sources do not link the two.\n\nThe link to the move is timing. Accounting questions arrived on a stock that had just posted a beat, and the shares fell hard within hours.",
