      "text": "Tuesday's results were strong on every number cited. Nvidia reported fiscal Q3 revenue of $115.8 billion, up 103% and above Wall Street's $109.2 billion estimate. Data center revenue rose 110% year over year to $107.6 billion. Adjusted earnings per share rose 111% to $2.68, above the $2.47 estimate. Fourth-quarter guidance of $130 billion, plus or minus 3%, beat expectations of about $125 billion. Motley Fool contributor Adam Spatacco also cited a fiscal 2028 revenue growth outlook raised to 75% from 70%.\n\nThe stock still could not hold a gain. Shares rose as much as 3% in extended trading, then sank about 3% on Wednesday. Luke Lango, editor of the Innovation Investor newsletter, told Bloomberg that none of it answers \"the fundamental, existential question facing the business right now.\"\n\nThe week also left open items. Board chairman Harvey Jones said the board does not intend to reinstate Jensen Huang as CEO. CFO Colette Kress is interim CEO, and Nvidia hired Heidrick & Struggles to find a permanent successor. Nvidia disclosed about $118 billion in financial guarantees and said its Form 10-Q would be filed in the coming weeks, not with earnings.",
